WEBVTT

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.640
Governance controls help teams avoid accidental waste without stopping useful engineering.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.900
Budgets make expected spend visible before it becomes a surprise.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:10.500
Alerts can warn owners when a project, environment,

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.900
or workload is trending beyond expectations.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.400
Tags are the language of ownership.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:18.900
A useful tagging plan connects resources to teams, projects, environments, cost centers,

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.435
owners, and lifecycle state.

00:00:20.435 --> 00:00:22.730
Without tags, cost review becomes guesswork.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:26.300
With tags, teams can connect spend to purpose and ask better questions.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:30.400
Quotas and default limits prevent accidental scale from becoming open-ended spend.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.790
They should be high enough to support legitimate work and low enough to force review

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:35.230
before expensive growth.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:38.930
Exception handling should be clear so urgent business needs have a responsible path.

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:41.300
Approval workflows should match risk and value.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.600
A small experiment may need lightweight controls.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.485
A production inference deployment or large training run may need stronger review.

00:00:47.485 --> 00:00:51.300
Guardrails are most effective when they block risky defaults, require ownership,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.125
and make cost impact visible.

00:00:53.125 --> 00:00:54.081
Engineers need feedback.

00:00:54.081 --> 00:00:57.735
If the people creating workloads cannot see spend, utilization, and ownership,

00:00:57.735 --> 00:00:59.300
they cannot improve behavior.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:03.230
Cost control works best when financial signals appear close to the technical choices that

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:03.940
create them.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:05.970
Guardrails should also be humane and practical.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.450
A blocked deployment should explain what rule failed, who can approve an exception,

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.100
and what safer default is expected.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.400
Clear feedback turns policy into guidance instead of friction for busy engineering teams.
